  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Between Monaco and Menton, Roquebrune-Cap-Martin reserves a section of the Golfe Bleu beach for dogs, at the western end, not far from the train station. The rest of the town's beaches, however, are closed to dogs.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Outside this dedicated area, dogs are banned from the beaches of Roquebrune-Cap-Martin — it's worth clearly spotting the boundaries of the authorised zone on arrival to avoid any issue. Point worth flagging: one general-purpose source claims the western area is no longer authorised for dogs at all (the beach fully off-limits), while other sources (wawaf.fr, emmenetonchien.com) instead confirm an on-leash reserved zone at the western end, consistent with what we state here. We can't settle this with certainty — it could reflect a recent change in the local bylaw: check on-site or with the town hall before travelling specifically for this.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: Monaco, right next door, has no beach open to dogs anyway — so make the most of the Golfe Bleu before or after a walk through the Principality.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Menton cani-plage is just a few kilometres east, a good option if you're combining several Riviera spots in the same day.</p>
